From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] gpio-button-hotplug: keep the fwnode_irq_get() error signed

Buttons never worked on any board whose gpio-keys child nodes have no
"interrupts" property - the probe logged, twice per board,

  gpio-keys keys: failed to request irq:0 for gpio:-2

(both printed values are artifacts: bdata->irq is zeroed just before the
dev_err, and button->gpio is the -ENOENT device-tree marker.)

struct gpio_keys_button::irq is unsigned int, so

	button->irq = fwnode_irq_get(pp, 0);
	if (button->irq < 0)
		button->irq = 0;

can never clamp: the comparison of an unsigned against 0 is always false
and the compiler drops it. That was harmless while fwnode_irq_get()
returned 0 for "no interrupt", but it now returns -EINVAL instead - see
"[PATCH v8 0/8] fix fwnode_irq_get[_byname()] returnvalue", which made
these helpers always report a negative errno so callers stop mistaking 0
for success. -EINVAL lands in the unsigned field as 4294967274, which is
nonzero, so gpio_keys_probe() takes the "interrupt supplied by firmware"
branch:

	if (!button->irq) {
		bdata->irq = gpiod_to_irq(bdata->gpiod);
		irqflags |= IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING | IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING;
	} else {
		bdata->irq = button->irq;
	}

skipping gpiod_to_irq() *and* leaving irqflags without any trigger type,
then requesting IRQ 4294967274, which fails.

Confirmed on an IPQ5018 board by printing the discarded values:

  DIAG: node='button-0' code=529 fwnode_irq_get=-22
  DIAG: -> button->irq=-22   (i.e. the clamp did not run)

while requesting the very same GPIO's interrupt with the flags the
gpiod_to_irq() branch would have used succeeds:

  irqtest: gpio=540 gpiod_to_irq=84
  irqtest: mode=0 request_threaded_irq ret=0

Hold the return value in a signed temporary so the error is seen.

This is a BACKPORT of upstream OpenWrt commit b0a03893cb52
("gpio-button-hotplug: fix signed issue", 2026-07-10), which fixes this
exactly the same way. This port pins OpenWrt at 25ee126 (2026-07-06),
four days earlier, so it does not have the fix yet.

DROP THIS PATCH once the OpenWrt pin advances past b0a03893 - it will
fail to apply, which is the intended signal that it is no longer needed.

--- a/gpio-button-hotplug.c
+++ b/gpio-button-hotplug.c
@@ -378,6 +378,7 @@
 
 	device_for_each_child_node_scoped(dev, pp) {
 		struct gpio_keys_button *button = &buttons[i++];
+		int irq;
 
 		if (fwnode_property_read_u32(pp, "linux,code", &button->code)) {
 			dev_err(dev, "Button node '%s' without keycode\n",
@@ -397,11 +398,20 @@
 
 		button->wakeup = fwnode_property_present(pp, "gpio-key,wakeup");
 		button->gpio = -ENOENT; /* mark this as device-tree */
-		button->irq = fwnode_irq_get(pp, 0);
-		if (button->irq == -EPROBE_DEFER)
-			return ERR_PTR(button->irq);
-		if (button->irq < 0)
-			button->irq = 0;
+		/*
+		 * struct gpio_keys_button::irq is unsigned, so the old
+		 * "if (button->irq < 0) button->irq = 0;" was dead code: a
+		 * negative errno was stored as a huge positive number and the
+		 * probe then treated it as a valid interrupt. Since
+		 * fwnode_irq_get() started returning -EINVAL rather than 0 when
+		 * a node has no "interrupts" property, that made every plain
+		 * gpio-keys node request a bogus IRQ and fail. Keep the error
+		 * in a signed temporary.
+		 */
+		irq = fwnode_irq_get(pp, 0);
+		if (irq == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+			return ERR_PTR(irq);
+		button->irq = max(0, irq);
 	}
 
 	pdata = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(struct gpio_keys_platform_data), GFP_KERNEL);
